@maintainer-2  Thanks for the suggestion, I was looking at dynamic batching because of some other project requirements - but I did generate a model with a fixed batch size. I exported with batch_size=5 (4 SAHI slices + 1 resized full image, so that I have one forward pass for all elements), then built the TensorRT engine with a matching optimization profile.

Batching works correctly, but the speedup is minimal. I wrote a diagnostic script that isolates raw TRT execute_async_v3 time (no python or postprocessing overhead) and measured that batching only gives ~13% latency improvement over running 5 sequential inferences, but scales near-linearly. 

Setup: RF-DETR XLarge segmentation, TensorRT FP16, 624 resolution, RTX 5070 (12 GB). The engine input shape is (5, 3, 1000, 630) with preprocessing (resize + normalize) baked into the ONNX graph.

 I expected larger improvements, is this expected for RF-DETR's architecture design?

@user-29  Curious if you've seen similar scaling behavior on

Thanks @user-30 for the example, that makes sense for your case.

Proposal: accept `threshold: float | list[float]` in `predict`. A list must have length `len(model.class_names)`, and each detection is kept when its score exceeds the threshold for its class. The float form is unchanged.

@maintainer-1, if this design works for you, could you please assign the issue to me ^^.

@maintainer-4 I haven't tested yet this export to CoreML but overall even though having `CoreMLExecutionProvider` in onnxruntime as delegate is nice to have from my experience with playing with different vision models onnxruntime with CoreML delegate was never as fast when model converted directly to CoreML.

I guess onnxruntime don't implement all supported ANE operators / conversions. Even when using onnxruntime tool (https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/tutorials/mobile/helpers/model-usability-checker.html) most of the time its showed some operators were not supported or there was too many partitions. 

For this reason I think it is still worth to have support for native CoreML models. And I think the end goal is to execute not just on MPS but on ANE (Apple neural engine) as many graph part as possible. I'm mostly interested on inference on smartphones that don't have many GPU cores but e.g. my old iphone 13 mini have still very fast NPU the same as my macbook m2 max.
